1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,837
Electromagnets are everywhere.

2
00:00:01,837 --> 00:00:08,474
A loudspeaker has a coil behind its cone: changing current makes a changing magnetic field that moves the cone,

3
00:00:08,474 --> 00:00:14,814
making sound. Electric bells, motors in blenders and fans, and computer hard drives all use electromagnets.

4
00:00:15,873 --> 00:00:21,740
In industry, giant electromagnets on cranes lift scrap iron and steel, then switch off to drop it.

5
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:25,888
Recycling plants use them to pull magnetic metals out of mixed waste.

6
00:00:25,888 --> 00:00:30,391
Power station generators use spinning electromagnets to produce electricity.

7
00:00:31,450 --> 00:00:36,487
In hospitals, MRI scanners use a huge magnet to line up hydrogen atoms in your body.

8
00:00:36,487 --> 00:00:40,991
Radio waves knock them out of line; as they flip back, they release energy,

9
00:00:40,991 --> 00:00:44,605
which the scanner uses to build detailed pictures inside you.

10
00:00:45,665 --> 00:00:50,227
Electromagnets are hidden inside many everyday things. Here are four of them.

11
00:00:51,287 --> 00:00:55,731
In an electric doorbell, pressing the button switches on an electromagnet.

12
00:00:55,731 --> 00:00:59,227
It pulls a metal striker onto the bell, and the bell rings.

13
00:01:00,286 --> 00:01:03,545
Some security doors are held shut by an electromagnet.

14
00:01:03,545 --> 00:01:07,812
When a card is accepted, the current is switched off and the door opens.

15
00:01:08,871 --> 00:01:14,204
In speakers and headphones, a changing current in a coil makes a changing magnetic field,

16
00:01:14,204 --> 00:01:16,278
which moves a cone and makes sound.

17
00:01:17,337 --> 00:01:21,959
At recycling centres, an electromagnet lifts steel cans out of mixed rubbish,

18
00:01:21,959 --> 00:01:23,974
leaving the aluminium cans behind.

19
00:01:25,033 --> 00:01:31,374
All of these work because an electromagnet can be switched on and off, and its strength can be controlled.

20
00:01:31,374 --> 00:01:34,573
Pause and think of one more device that might use one.

21
00:01:35,633 --> 00:01:41,144
Now it is your turn to shine! Pause the video after each question and try to answer it first.

22
00:01:42,203 --> 00:01:47,417
Question 1: What happens when two North poles are brought together? Pause the video now.

23
00:01:48,477 --> 00:01:49,602
Answer: They repel.

24
00:01:50,662 --> 00:01:54,395
Question 2: Name three magnetic materials. Pause the video now.

25
00:01:55,454 --> 00:01:58,121
Answer: Iron, nickel and cobalt (also steel).

26
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:03,980
Question 3: Give two ways to make an electromagnet stronger. Pause the video now.

27
00:02:05,039 --> 00:02:08,002
Answer: More turns, more current, or an iron core.

28
00:02:09,061 --> 00:02:15,224
Question 4: Why are electromagnets used in scrapyards instead of permanent magnets? Pause the video now.

29
00:02:16,283 --> 00:02:19,305
Answer: They can be switched off to drop the metal.
